---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import Capture from '../components/Capture.astro'
import SurfaceLab from '../components/SurfaceLab.astro'
import { connectors, doc, packages, releases, repo, shot, subscriptions } from '../lib/content'
---

<Page>
  <section class="hero container" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open-source desktop app for the pi coding agent</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Ten agents. <br />Ten branches. <br /><span>One window.</span></h1>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-summary">
      <p class="hero-lede">
        Give every task its own agent and its own Git worktree. Watch them run, read the diffs,
        merge what’s good.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the models you already pay for: Claude, ChatGPT, Copilot, Kimi, or any API key pi
        supports. Add pi extensions when you need more than code.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="action action-primary" href="/guides/"
          >Get started <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
        >
        <a class="action action-text" href="#work">How it works <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a
        >
      </div>
      <div class="hero-meta mono">
        <span>macOS / Linux / Windows</span><span>Open source · MIT</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-proof">
      <Capture
        image={shot('home')}
        alt="Phosphor’s lane dashboard with parallel sessions, a ready-to-merge task, and project usage."
        caption="Every lane at a glance, and what’s ready to merge"
        sizes="(max-width: 480px) 92vw, (max-width: 1422px) 90vw, 1280px"
        eager
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="work" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">01 / How it works</p><h2>
            Start with<br />a chat.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Type what you want done. Every tool call and edit shows up as it happens, and each chat
          runs on its own branch.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Capture
        class="workflow-chat"
        image={shot('chat')}
        alt="Phosphor’s chat-focused layout with a session sidebar, conversation, inline edit, and composer, with no auxiliary pane open."
        caption="Chat first, with the agent’s work one click away"
      />
      <div class="numbered-notes">
        <article>
          <span>01 / ISOLATE</span><h3>One task, one branch.</h3><p>
            Open a folder, pick a model, and the session starts in its own Git worktree. Start a
            second task and it gets a separate checkout.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article id="transcript">
          <span>02 / INSPECT</span><h3>See what it actually did.</h3><p>
            Expand any tool call for its arguments and output. Open an edit for its diff. Review
            every changed file against where the session started, and revert what you don’t want.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article id="artifacts">
          <span>03 / KEEP</span><h3>Plans and docs stick around.</h3><p>
            Plans, diagrams, and HTML pages the agent writes are saved as versioned artifacts.
            Reopen the session later to read, edit, or compare versions.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="text-link" href="/guides/#first-task">Run your first task →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href={doc('lanes.md')}>How lanes and PRs work ↗</a
        ><a class="text-link" href={doc('extensions.md#the-artifact-tools-and-what-each-one-costs')}
          >How artifacts work ↗</a
        >
      </div>
      <SurfaceLab />
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>Changes and artifacts <span>+</span></summary><div class="capture-pair">
          <Capture
            image={shot('changes')}
            alt="An inline edit with the Changes pane beside the conversation."
            caption="Review the agent’s edits"
          />
          <Capture
            image={shot('artifacts')}
            alt="A document in the versioned artifact pane beside its conversation."
            caption="Artifacts open next to the chat"
          />
        </div><p class="fineprint">
          PR status comes from the gh CLI. Merges happen locally with git, not through GitHub.
          Artifact HTML runs isolated with no network access. That isolation covers the page, not
          the agent’s tools.
        </p>
      </details>
      <p class="fineprint" id="sessions">
        Resume old sessions, fork a conversation, or queue a follow-up while the agent is still
        working. For recurring work, <a class="text-link" href={doc('routines.md')}
          >local routines</a
        >
        start a new session on a schedule. They run on your machine while it’s awake, not in the cloud.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="models" class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">02 / Models and accounts</p><h2>
            Use the plans<br />you already pay for.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          Sign in with a subscription, add an API key, or point at your own endpoint. Switch models
          mid-session without losing the conversation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="subscription-grid">
        {
          subscriptions.map(({ name, plan, route }) => (
            <article>
              <>
                <h3>{name}</h3>
                <p class="mono">{plan}</p>
                <p>{route}</p>
              </>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="split feature-detail">
        <div class="copy-column">
          <h3 class="subheading">More than one<br />Claude account.</h3><p>
            Add every Claude login you have. New sessions can use one account, go down the list in
            order, or rotate round robin. A running session stays on the account it started with.
          </p><p>
            The context meter shows tokens and cost as reported, an estimate of what’s filling the
            context, and how much of your Claude plan is left. Provider limits still apply.
          </p><a class="action action-text" href="/guides/claude-code/"
            >Set up Claude and multiple accounts →</a
          ><a class="text-link" href={doc('chat.md#what-the-context-meters-popover-shows')}
            >How the context meter works ↗</a
          >
        </div>
        <Capture
          image={shot('models')}
          alt="Searchable models grouped by provider in Phosphor’s composer."
          caption="Pick the model for the next turn"
        />
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">
        Claude subscriptions need the Claude Code CLI and the pi-claude-cli package. Your provider
        bills you for subscriptions and API usage. Phosphor itself is free. <a
          class="text-link"
          href="/guides/#sign-in">Pick a sign-in route →</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="extensions" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">03 / Extensions</p><h2>
            Coding works out of the box.<br />Install the rest.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          pi runs the agent loop. Packages add tools on top: web search, MCP services, subagents,
          computer use. Phosphor installs, updates, and configures them from Settings.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="package-grid">
        {
          packages.map(({ name, benefit, id }) => (
            <a class="package-card" href={`/guides/extensions/#${id}`}>
              <>
                <span class="mono">OPTIONAL PI PACKAGE</span>
                <h3>
                  {name} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
                </h3>
                <p>{benefit}</p>
              </>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="action action-primary" href="/guides/extensions/">Set up extensions →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://pi.dev/packages">Browse pi packages ↗</a
        >
      </div>
      <aside class="boundary-note">
        <strong>Built in vs. installed</strong><p>
          Artifacts, the context breakdown, and a few other extensions ship with Phosphor. The
          packages above are optional: install them in Settings → Extensions, then start a new
          session. Packages run code with your permissions, so check what you install.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="connectors" class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <span id="gateways"></span>
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">04 / Connectors</p><h2>
            Your tools,<br />in the same session.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          Install the MCP adapter and connect the services you want the agent to use. No more
          pasting tickets and logs into the chat.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="connector-grid">
        {
          connectors.map(({ name, use, id }) => (
            <a href={`/guides/connectors/#${id}`}>
              <>
                <h3>
                  {name} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
                </h3>
                <p>{use}</p>
              </>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <Capture
        image={shot('guide-connectors')}
        alt="MCP Connectors settings with a connected-server list and the catalog of available services, using example configuration."
        caption="Add, sign in, test, or reconnect from Settings"
        provenance="Settings demo · example data"
      />
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="action action-primary" href="/guides/connectors/">Connect your first service →</a
        ><a class="text-link" href="/guides/connectors/#custom">Add a custom MCP server →</a>
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">
        Setting up and testing a connector doesn’t call a model. Using its tools in a task does, and
        may count against the service’s quota. The adapter stores OAuth tokens, not Phosphor. Slack
        needs your own registered Slack app.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="section install-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p><h2>Install it.<br />Give it a real task.</h2>
        </div><div class="copy-column">
          <p class="section-intro">
            Install pi and Phosphor, sign in to a provider, and add extensions when you need them.
            The guides walk through the real settings screens.
          </p><a class="action action-primary" href="/guides/">Install guide →</a><a
            class="text-link"
            href={`${releases}/latest`}>Download the latest release ↗</a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="guide-grid">
        <a href="/guides/claude-code/"
          ><h3>Claude Code + accounts ↗</h3><p>
            Install the provider, sign in, test it, and route new sessions.
          </p></a
        ><a href="/guides/extensions/"
          ><h3>Choose your extensions ↗</h3><p>
            What’s built in, what to install, and how packages load.
          </p></a
        ><a href="/guides/connectors/"
          ><h3>Connect your services ↗</h3><p>
            MCP setup, permissions, sign-in, testing, and custom servers.
          </p></a
        >
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">
        MIT licensed. Runs on your machine, not as a hosted service. The agent’s tools have full
        permissions by default, and your providers see whatever you send them. <a
          class="text-link"
          href="/guides/#trust">Permissions and data →</a
        >
      </p>
      <div class="source-line" id="feature-index">
        <a class="text-link" href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/README.md`}>Technical docs ↗</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href={`${repo}/issues`}>Report a bug or ask for help ↗</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
